      "title": "A generic “preserve editorial intent” prompt can reduce, not improve, editorial fidelity",
      "description": "Adding a reusable Narrative instruction to “preserve editorial intent” produced consistent editorial drift: company identity and audience scope were deprioritized, orchestration became the central message, unsupported claims appeared, and the candidate prompt failed promotion. The experiment shows that a single, generic preservation rule can worsen downstream outputs rather than protect a supplied hierarchy.",
      "audience": "Prompt engineers, content ops leads, product/content managers responsible for automated content pipelines",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Teams testing prompt-level editorial safeguards need to know that a plausible, reusable rule can produce systematic regressions; relying on a single generic rule without targeted alignment to the existing Synthesis risks displacing the intended top-level message. Gaps and uncertainty: the experiment used three runs per condition (not statistically conclusive) and the result may depend on exact wording, model backend variability, or Synthesis content not modified in this experiment.",
      "evidence": "Candidate prompt added a new “Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis” section. Narrative mean score fell from 6.00 to 4.00/10; Compose mean fell from 5.67 to 3.33/10. All three candidate Narrative runs subordinated company identity/audience and made orchestration central; all candidate runs introduced unsupported claims and failed the D (factual/editorial restraint) gate. Decision: candidate not promoted; production prompt restored byte-for-byte."
    },
    {
      "title": "Enforce a hard editorial-restraint gate: unsupported expansions are a categorical failure",
      "description": "The experiment treated factual/editorial restraint (no unsupported audience, capability, launch, or commitment claims) as a hard promotion gate. The candidate prompt repeatedly triggered unsupported additions (validation intent, named audience segments, implied commitments), causing automatic rejection—demonstrating that strict restraint criteria are effective failure signals in pipeline evaluation.",
      "audience": "Content governance, compliance teams, editorial reviewers, pipeline QA",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Having an explicit, hard gate for unsupported claims gives a clear rejection criterion for automated edits and prompt changes; experiments that pass readability and coherence can still be unsafe if they introduce factual or commitment-level assertions. Gaps and uncertainty: the report shows failure cases but does not evaluate automated detection alternatives or remediation strategies.",
      "evidence": "Scoring rubric defines D (factual/editorial restraint) as a hard gate. Candidate Narrative and Compose runs repeatedly scored 0 on D due to introduced validation intent and named audience claims. The final decision cites failing the restraint gate in every run as a primary reason not to promote the candidate."
    },
    {
      "title": "A controlled A/B rubric (A–E plus fidelity metrics) provides a practical method to evaluate editorial prompt changes",
      "description": "The experiment used a reproducible baseline vs candidate comparison with explicit scoring criteria (A–E), plus readability, coherence, and source-fidelity ratings, to evaluate whether a prompt change should be promoted. This structured approach makes trade-offs visible and supports an evidence-based promotion decision.",
      "audience": "Prompt engineering teams, ML ops, product QA, researchers running evaluation experiments",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Teams need repeatable, interpretable evaluation frameworks when changing prompts in production; the documented rubric and run-level scoring give clear pass/fail signals and diagnose which editorial dimensions regressed. Gaps and uncertainty: the experiment used three runs per condition and no temperature/seed control; it notes model-alias backend variability as a limitation.",
      "evidence": "Experiment compared three baseline runs reused from prior experiment and three candidate runs; used gpt-5-mini; scored A–E (identity/audience, first product direction, theme hierarchy, factual restraint (hard gate), LinkedIn suitability) each 0–2, plus readability/coherence/source-fidelity 1–5. Report contains run-by-run tables and aggregated means used to decide not to promote."
